# dependencies
node_modules

# env files
.env
.env.*

# misc
.DS_Store
.vscode
README

.vite/

# ...but the example MUST be committed: it documents the required vars, and a
# missing one is what produced the white screen. The negation has to sit on its
# own line - glued onto `.vite/` it never applied, so `.env.*` was quietly
# ignoring `.env.example` too.
!.env.example

# Vite build output (this project outputs to build/, not dist/).
build

# Playwright and probe output. These are generated by running the suite and the
# browser probes from this directory, and they have no business in the repo --
# both of this project's past secret leaks were in files an extension-filtered
# scan walked past, so artifacts get named explicitly rather than trusted to
# stay out on their own.
test-results/
playwright-report/
.playwright-mcp/
*-probe.png
admin-login.png

# secrets and keys - none of these exist in this repo today, and the point is
# that none of them can ever stage by accident either. This block is copied from
# the backend's .gitignore, which has carried it all along: this repo was the one
# missing it, so the three repos that ship together were not equally covered.
# Cheap to add; the failure it prevents is not recoverable once pushed.
secrets/
prod.env
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*credentials*.json
id_rsa
# `token.json` is the name every OAuth helper and CLI writes its refresh token
# to, and it does not match `*credentials*.json`. The admin repo has carried this
# line all along; this repo and the backend did not, so of the three repos that
# ship together only one was covered. Verified with a real decoy file on disk
# (`git check-ignore -v token.json`), not against a pattern — nothing of the sort
# exists in this tree, and the point is that it can no longer arrive by accident.
token*.json
